    "insert": "Review: Giving intelligence a Face",
    "attributes": {
      "size": "large"
    }
  },
  {
    "insert": "\n\nThe given exposé proposes research into the reception of different kinds of appearances for holographic assistants\nbased on artificial intelligence (AI), including ethical questions, to define a guideline for these systems. They are\nsupposed to provide an alternative to current text based and graphical user interfaces (GUIs) in the hope of increasing\nthe adoption rate of AI assistants.\n\nStarting off with strengths: your exposé manages to communicate key arguments precisely. You list necessary citations\nand facts, lay them out and elaborate on them and make sensible conclusions from those. Such cases are marked\ngreen in CARE to not bloat the review up. Here are two sections worthy of a highlight.\nThe anchor in the motivation should give context about the problem and describes previous attempts at solving it. The\nsection juxtaposes the rapid progress of AI technologies with studies about AI chatbot usage rates, raising questions\nabout this disparity and correctly identifying a analogous development with GUIs, convincingly transitioning into this \nnext aspect and focusing on its role of technology adoption. At last the section makes a case for holographic AI.\nThis lays out the solid groundwork for your upcoming research project, making clear that there is a historical\nprecedent.\nThe other highlight can be found in the approach. The state of the art should collect works done in the area and\nposition your project. Again, you cite various papers and put their key statements against each other, ending with a\nreasonable conclusion for your research and reuse of some findings there for a theoretical framework. All in all, this\nmakes for a compelling line of thought and sets up domain knowledge for your methodology.\n\nMoving on to weaknesses: the hook of your motivation should give the reader a first impression about the extent of an\nproblem or describe some vision the project wants to realise. It consists of very simple statements creating a bridge\nbetween the topic and fiction but after that, the text does not capitalise on this idea. Instead, various statements\nabout the current state of AI affairs are made, dismissing your hook and not fulfilling one's expectations. Try\ncreating a bridge between fiction and reality by elaborating the steps towards realising them. This requires an\nexpansion of the hook and anchor in this section.\nA crucial aspect of the motivation is the teaser that sets the scope of the project with realistic and clear research \nquestions (RQs). The RQs are not clearly written out; beginning with something that looks like a research question\n(\"[...] if a holographic AI assistant is beneficial to users and [...]\"), from which the text backs down from by \nnarrowing the scope down to investigating the look and feel of such system with user interaction. After that, there \nis some context about this more suited in the anchor prior and, at last, it jumps to declaring the discussion of \nethical questions and the creation of a guideline for holographic AI systems as RQs. This deludes the line of thought\nin the paragraph and leaves the reader with unclear expectations going into the approach. Their broad nature reinforces\nthis problem, e.g. wanting to assess how an assistant should look and sound cannot be done in general, only on a per \nuse basis. Rewrite the RQs into bullet points, move explanations up to the anchor and redefine the focus for your\nresearch.\n\nOn that topic, we will focus on the methodology. It should describe a credible way, i.e. research possible in the 24 \nweek time frame of a thesis, and sensible methods towards answering the research questions. The need for a testing\nplatform is stated, sensible at first glance. The problem herein lies in the implication that this makes the time\nneeded unpredictable; there are no arguments for this notion in this exposé. From this statement onwards, multiple\npossible models are listed, diluting the certainty and confidence in the project. If such consideration of uncertainty\nis absolutely necessary, than it must be elaborated. Furthermore, scale down on the assistants used and be certain to\nuse them; this may include arguments about technical feasibility, to mention an example.\nAnother note about the methodology will put a focus on the study process. As already stated, the section should contain\nmethods making progress to answer RQs. Some of those are of questionable use, like adjusting the assistant's LLM \nparameters during the study in multiple different ways or heart rate monitoring of participants; they lack\nsufficient justification in the context of your research, especially a description about potential effects on results.\nAdding the missing context with respect to the RQs with a focus on connecting methods and theory would legitimize\nthe methods.\nTo make a case for your research, the exposé require precise methods for substantial evaluation. Besides the deficits\nabove, some parts of the study are only covered in shallow terms; examples are marked in CARE, found on page 4. They\nall share the fact that they do not make use of the given theoretical framework, especially metrics. This make \nevaluation difficult, thus the research is at risk. Elaborating on the methods as processes and connecting them to the\ntheory is highly recommended.\n\nAt last, some miscellaneous notes. The schedule should be a detailed and precise plan for conducting your proposed\nresearch. It contains some parts that are uncertain, like an undefined amount of study participants, which pose a risk\nin the project. Clear up such uncertainties as much as possible.\nA scientific text should be formatted, have a good language style and proper citation style. That is dubious at some\nparts: The text lacks paragraphs which would separate lines of thought from each other, making it hard to read. The\nabbreviation HCI, Human-Computer-Interaction, is misspelled with \"hci\", and citations appear multiple times in the same\nsentence or are cluttered together. Other things like typos are marked in CARE.\n\nAll in all, the exposé makes convincing arguments for research into holographic AI assistants, especially citing a\nsimilar development prior (GUIs), demonstrating a vision. It is sadly falling short in formulating substantial research\nquestions of planning the research out to gather usable results.\n\n"
  }
]